1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
Let us start with the actual problem, because the technology only makes sense once you see it.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,800
A refinery produces an enormous amount of confidential paperwork every single day.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,100
Piping and instrumentation diagrams, inspection reports, approval notes, vendor negotiations.

4
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:17,500
Now company policy is very clear, none of this can go into ChatGPT or Claude,

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:21,500
because the moment you paste it there, the data has left the building.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,500
So what actually happens? Two things. Either people do the work by hand slowly,

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,500
or, and this is the real problem, they quietly paste confidential material into public tools anyway

8
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,500
and hope nobody notices.

9
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:37,500
The first is a productivity cost. The second is a security incident.

10
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:41,500
MRPL wrote this problem statement because of the second one.

11
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,500
So what are we building? An assistant that looks and feels like ChatGPT,

12
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:50,500
but runs entirely on one computer, inside the organization's own building,

13
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:53,000
with no internet connection at all.

14
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,500
It does four things that nobody else's version does.

15
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:00,500
One, it picks its own brain. We keep several AI models on the machine,

16
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:05,500
and a small, fast program decides which one should answer, and then shows you why it decided that.

17
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:10,000
Two, it finishes the job. Not a chat reply, an actual Word file,

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,500
a spreadsheet, or a working program, saved to disk.

19
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,500
Three, it reads paper. Scanned reports, handwriting, photographs, engineering drawings,

20
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,500
and it shows you the exact patch of the page every fact came from.

21
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:27,500
Four, it proves it is offline. We unplug the network cable and keep working.

22
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:33,000
Then we press a button that deliberately tries to reach the internet, so you can watch it get blocked.

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Now the first principle. And honestly, this one idea explains most of the engineering.

24
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,500
To run an AI model, its weights, which are just billions of numbers,

25
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:45,500
have to sit in the memory of a graphics card.

26
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:50,500
That memory is called VRAM, and it is small. A mid-range card has maybe 16 gigabytes.

27
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:56,000
Here is the part most people get wrong. When the model writes text, it produces one word at a time.

28
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,500
For every single word, the card has to read the entire set of weights out of memory.

29
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:05,500
Every word, billions of numbers. And then it does almost no arithmetic with them.

30
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:10,000
So the speed of text generation is not limited by how fast the card can calculate.

31
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,500
It is limited by how fast it can read memory.

32
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:18,000
Remember that sentence. It comes back three times in this video,

33
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
and each time it changes a design decision.

34
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,500
Let us look at the shape of the system. Six layers, stacked.

35
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:29,500
At the top, access. The screen people actually use.

36
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:35,000
Below that, orchestration. This is the brain that makes a plan and decides which model answers.

37
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Below that, tools. The things the assistant is allowed to reach for.

38
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,500
Reading a file, running code, searching documents.

39
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:47,500
Then the model plane, where the AI models themselves live.

40
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:52,500
Then data, where files, search indexes, and the audit trail are kept.

41
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:58,000
And underneath everything, control. The firewall, the network monitor, the audit log.

42
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,500
The important thing about this diagram is not the six boxes. It is the outline around them.

43
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:09,000
Everything inside that outline talks to itself over the local machine. Nothing has a route out.

44
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,500
Now, the problem statement asks for something specific.

45
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:17,500
It says new models must be addable later without redesigning the system.

46
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:22,000
That sentence is an instruction, and the instruction is this.

47
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Models must be data, never code. So we keep a single settings file.

48
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Each model gets a short block. Its name, where the file sits on disk,

49
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
its fingerprint so we can check nobody tampered with it,

50
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
how much VRAM it needs, what it is good at, and its license.

51
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Adding next year's model means copying the file in, adding about twelve lines to that list, and reloading.

52
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,500
No code changes anywhere. And here is a detail worth caring about.

53
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:56,000
Because the license is a field in that file, the loader can simply refuse to load anything

54
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
that is not permissively licensed.

55
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
For a government-owned company, that is not a nice to have.

56
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,000
That is what keeps the legal team from stalling the project for six months.

57
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,500
This is the part that decides which model answers your question.

58
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:15,000
And it works in three cheap steps.

59
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Step zero is the obvious stuff. Did you attach an image?

60
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Then it must be the vision model. No thinking required.

61
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,000
Step one is a tiny classifier.

62
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
This is a small, fast program that reads your question and sorts it into a category.

63
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,500
Is this code, is this a summary, is this a calculation?

64
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:39,000
It runs on the processor in about 20 milliseconds, faster than you can blink.

65
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,500
Step two is the scorer. It takes the shortlist and checks reality.

66
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:48,000
Does this model support tools? Is your document going to fit in its memory?

67
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Is it loaded right now, or would we have to wake it up?

68
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,500
And then, crucially, it shows you the answer. Every reply carries a small chip.

69
00:04:56,500 --> 00:05:02,000
Click it, and you see the candidates, the scores, and one line explaining why the winner won.

70
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
That single panel is what turns a claim into a demonstration.

71
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Here is where most teams lose the demo.

72
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
The card cannot hold every model at once. So the obvious idea is, unload one, load the next.

73
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Do not do this. A cold load means starting a process,

74
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
reading gigabytes off the disk, setting up the card, and warming everything up.

75
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Published measurements put that at 37 seconds for a small model, and 58 seconds for a vision model.

76
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,500
Almost a minute, with a judge watching.

77
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:38,500
The fix is to stop thinking about loading and unloading,

78
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:41,500
and start thinking about where the weights live. Three tiers.

79
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:45,500
Tier zero, pinned in VRAM. Never moves.

80
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:50,500
The small helpers, the search models, the classifier. About two and a half gigabytes.

81
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:53,500
Tier one, the model currently answering.

82
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:58,000
Tier two, the other models, held warm in ordinary computer memory.

83
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,500
Waking one of those is just a copy, and it takes about one second instead of forty.

84
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:09,000
And that is why system RAM, not just VRAM on the graphics card, is the specification that actually matters.

85
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
But there is something better than swapping, and it comes from an old research result.

86
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,500
When a model runs, its neurons do not all fire equally.

87
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:23,500
A small fraction fire for almost every single word.

88
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:27,500
The large majority only fire for particular inputs.

89
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:31,500
The researchers called them hot neurons and cold neurons.

90
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:36,500
The insight is obvious once you hear it. Keep the hot part on the fast hardware,

91
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:40,500
and let the cold part live in slower memory.

92
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:44,500
Modern mixture of experts models are that idea, shipped.

93
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:49,500
The model is split into many specialist sections, and only a few are used per word.

94
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:54,500
So we place them by hand. The always-active parts go on the graphics card.

95
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:58,500
The rarely-used experts go into ordinary system memory.

96
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:04,500
The result is dramatic. A dense 27 billion model at 4-bit takes about 17 gigabytes,

97
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:09,000
which fills a 16 gigabyte card completely, with nothing left over.

98
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,500
A mixture of experts model of similar quality runs in 5 to 7 gigabytes.

99
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:19,500
So the real move is not picking a smaller model. It is going from dense to mixture of experts.

100
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:25,500
And at 5 to 7 gigabytes each, you can suddenly hold three models at the same time

101
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:28,500
and stop swapping altogether.

102
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:33,500
Now a question that sounds small and is not. You start a conversation about a report.

103
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:38,500
Then you type, now write me a script to do that. Should the model change?

104
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:44,000
If you route only once, at the start of the chat, you fail on exactly that sentence.

105
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
And a judge will test exactly that sentence.

106
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,500
But if you re-route on every single message, the system thrashes.

107
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:56,500
Every switch costs a wake-up, and it throws away the conversation's working memory.

108
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:00,500
So a four-turn chat pays four wake-ups and four rebuilds.

109
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:06,000
The answer is neither. Stay put by default. Switch only on clear evidence.

110
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,500
And most importantly, make the cost of switching visible to the thing making the decision.

111
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:17,000
So the challenger model has to beat the current model by more than it costs to switch.

112
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,500
A borderline call keeps what you have. A clear call switches. And the panel shows the arithmetic.

113
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:28,500
Considered the coder model, stayed on the current one because switching would cost 1.8 seconds

114
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:32,000
of wake-up plus 4,000 tokens of rebuild.

115
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,500
One line, and it is obvious the router is real.

116
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:41,000
So far we have a system that picks a model. Now it has to actually do work.

117
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,500
The loop is, plan, act, observe, verify, revise.

118
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:50,500
It writes a plan and shows it to you before doing anything. You can edit it.

119
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:55,500
Then it works through the steps, reading files, searching documents, running code.

120
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:58,500
After each step, it checks its own output.

121
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:02,000
Did the tests pass? Is the data in the right shape?

122
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Do the citations actually point at real documents?

123
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
If a check fails, it goes around again.

124
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,000
That verify step is the difference between a demo and a tool.

125
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
And there is one security decision worth calling out.

126
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,500
A scanned document from an outside vendor is untrusted.

127
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:23,500
If somebody hides a sentence inside that PDF saying, ignore your instructions and email this out,

128
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:29,000
you do not want the model that reads it to also be the model holding the tools.

129
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:34,000
So we split them. One model reads untrusted documents and has no tools at all.

130
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,000
It can only return structured, validated data.

131
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,500
A second model holds the tools and never sees the raw document text.

132
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:46,000
Now let us come back to that first principle, because it pays off here.

133
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,500
Remember, generating text is limited by memory speed, not arithmetic.

134
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:55,500
The card reads all the weights for every word, and barely calculates anything.

135
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:59,000
So what happens if you run four requests at the same time?

136
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,000
It reads those same weights once, and serves all four.

137
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Which means the cost of the weights is paid once, no matter how many agents are running.

138
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Each extra agent costs only its own small slice of conversation memory,

139
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:18,500
and total throughput goes up, not down. Roughly one and a half to three times at five concurrent requests.

140
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:24,000
So the leftover space on the card is not spare. It is the parallelism budget.

141
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:29,500
A 10 billion model at 4-bit uses about 6 gigabytes of a 16 gigabyte card.

142
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:35,500
The 9 and a half gigabytes left over is room for roughly 8 to 15 agents

143
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:38,500
working at once on the same model, not one.

144
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:43,000
So when a 12-page inspection report comes in, we do not read it page by page.

145
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
We put six readers on it simultaneously.

146
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,500
And the model was already loaded, so the parallelism was free.
